      "text": "The given spherical coordinates are $\\left( \\rho,\\theta,\\phi \\right) = \\left( 3, \\frac{5\\pi}{12}, 0 \\right).$\n\nWe can convert to rectangular coordinates using the following equations:\n\\[(x, y, z) = \\left( \\rho \\sin \\phi \\cos \\theta, \\rho \\sin \\phi \\sin \\theta, \\rho \\cos \\phi \\right).\\]\n\nSubstituting the given values, we find:\n\\[(x, y, z) = \\left( 3 \\sin (0) \\cos \\left( \\frac{5\\pi}{12} \\right), 3 \\sin (0) \\sin \\left( \\frac{5\\pi}{12} \\right), 3 \\cos (0) \\right).\\]\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\\[(x, y, z) = \\left( 0, 0, 3 \\right).\\]\n\nTherefore, the rectangular coordinates are $\\boxed{(0, 0, 3)}.$\n\nTo convert from spherical coordinates to rectangular coordinates, we use the following equations:\n\n\\[(x, y, z) = \\left( \\rho \\sin \\phi \\cos \\theta, \\rho \\sin \\phi \\sin \\theta, \\rho \\cos \\phi \\right).\\]\n\nHere, \\(\\rho\\) is the radial distance, \\(\\theta\\) is the azimuthal angle, and \\(\\phi\\) is the polar angle. Since the problem doesn't ask us to find \\(\\phi\\) in this case, we can just focus on \\(\\theta\\) and \\(\\phi\\).\n\nGiven spherical coordinates \\((\\rho, \\theta, \\phi) = \\left( 3, \\frac{5\\pi}{12}, 0 \\right)\\), we plug them into the conversion formulas:\n\n- \\(\\rho\\) is 3.\n- \\(\\theta\\) is \\(\\frac{5\\pi}{12}\\).\n- \\(\\phi\\) is 0, which makes the \\(\\cos\\phi\\) term = 1.\n\nNow we calculate \\(x\\), \\(y\\), and \\(z\\):\n\n\\[\\begin{align*}\nx &= \\rho \\sin \\phi \\cos \\theta \\\\\n&= 3 \\sin(0) \\cos \\left( \\frac{5\\pi}{12} \\right) \\\\\n&= 0,\n\ny &= \\rho \\sin \\phi \\sin \\theta \\\\\n&= 3 \\sin(0) \\sin \\left( \\frac{5\\pi}{12} \\right) \\\\\n&= 0,\n\nz &= \\rho \\cos \\phi \\\\\n&= 3 \\cos(0) \\\\\n&= 3.\n\\end{align*}\\]\n\nThus, the rectangular coordinates are \\((x, y, z) = \\boxed{(0, 0, 3)}\\).",
